\label{lem:proj_diff}
Let $f(\theta):\mathbb R^k \rightarrow \mathbb R$ be a continuously differentiable convex function. Choose a constant $\delta>0$. Let $\theta_i$ be an interior point of $\mathcal D_\delta$, defined in \eqref{def:d}. Choose $\theta_b$ as a boundary point so that $f(\theta_b)=\delta$. Then the following holds:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:grad_geo}(\theta_i-\theta_b)^T \nabla f(\theta_b)\leq0\end{equation}
where $\nabla f(\theta_b)=\left(\frac{\partial f(\theta)}{\partial \theta_1 }\; \cdots \; \frac{\partial f(\theta)}{\partial \theta_k } \right)^T $ evaluated at $\theta_b$.

